  "id": "reflexive-verben",
  "name": "Reflexive verbs: je me lève, il s'habille",
  "level": "A2",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "variety": "fr-FR",
  "subtitle": "Many daily-routine verbs need a small pronoun that matches the subject: je me lève, tu te laves, elle s'habille. In the dictionary the verb starts with se: se lever. The pronoun goes right before the verb.",
  "briefMarks": ["me", "te", "se", "nous", "vous", "m'", "t'", "s'", "ne me … pas"],
  "source": "icanspeak French original text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-11-08)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [{"text": "(ne)"},{"text": "me · te · se · nous · vous · se", "variant": "prep"},{"text": "lève · laves · couche", "variant": "verb"},{"text": "(pas)"}],
    "exampleHtml": "Je <span class=\"vp-prep\">me</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">lève</span> à six heures. · Mon fils <span class=\"vp-prep\">s'</span><span class=\"vp-verb\">habille</span> seul. · À quelle heure est-ce que tu <span class=\"vp-prep\">te</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">couches</span> ? · Je <span class=\"vp-prep\">ne</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">me</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">repose</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">pas</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "In the dictionary the verb starts with <strong>se</strong>: <em>se lever, se laver, se coucher</em>. When you use it, the pronoun changes with the subject: <em>je me lève, tu te lèves, il se lève, nous nous levons, vous vous levez, ils se lèvent</em>.",
        "tag": "The form"
      },
      {
        "html": "Before a vowel or a mute <em>h</em>, <strong>me, te, se</strong> become <strong>m', t', s'</strong>: <em>je m'habille, tu t'appelles, elle s'occupe de ses enfants.</em> Not: <em>je me habille</em>.",
        "tag": "m', t', s'"
      },
      {
        "html": "In the negative, <strong>ne</strong> goes before the pronoun and <strong>pas</strong> after the verb: <em>Je ne me lève pas tôt. Elle ne s'habille pas.</em> The pronoun stays glued to the verb.",
        "tag": "Negative"
      },
      {
        "html": "With an <strong>infinitive</strong>, the pronoun goes before the infinitive, and it still matches the subject: <em>Je vais me coucher. Nous allons nous dépêcher. Tu veux te reposer ?</em>",
        "tag": "With an infinitive"
      },
      {
        "html": "For your own body, use <em>le, la, les</em>, not <em>mon, ma, mes</em>: <em>Je me lave les mains. Elle se brosse les dents.</em> Some verbs are always reflexive: <em>s'appeler, se dépêcher, s'occuper de</em>.",
        "tag": "Body, fixed verbs"
      },
      {
        "html": "Just a preview: in the past, reflexive verbs use <em>être</em>: <em>Je me suis levé(e) à six heures.</em> In the imperative the pronoun moves behind the verb: <em>Lève-toi !</em> You will practise both later.",
        "tag": "Later"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>Je lève à six heures</em> → ✓ <em>Je me lève à six heures</em>. ✗ <em>Je me habille</em> → ✓ <em>Je m'habille</em>. ✗ <em>Je me lave mes mains</em> → ✓ <em>Je me lave les mains</em>. ✗ <em>Je vais se coucher</em> → ✓ <em>Je vais me coucher</em>. ✗ <em>Je me ne lève pas</em> → ✓ <em>Je ne me lève pas</em>. Next: <a href=\"/ne-pas\">negation</a>, <a href=\"/imperative\">the imperative</a>, <a href=\"/passe-compose-avoir\">the passé composé</a>.",
